<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia</h1><p class="st-meta">1992 CE – 2003 CE · capital Belgrade</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Federation of Serbia and Montenegro formed in 1992 from the remains of Yugoslavia, dominated by Slobodan Milošević and marked by sanctions and war.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia</a> broke apart, the two remaining republics, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia-and-montenegro">Serbia and Montenegro</a>, adopted a new constitution on 27 April 1992 creating the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. It claimed to be the continuation of the old state, a claim most other states and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> rejected. The writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrica-cosic">Dobrica Ćosić</a> was its first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Real power lay with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a>, president of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> and from 1997 of the federation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> over the wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> brought hyperinflation in 1993 and economic collapse. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayton-agreement">Dayton Agreement</a> of 1995 came conflict in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> bombing in 1999 and Kosovo's placement under UN administration. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milo-dukanovic">Milo Đukanović</a> increasingly went its own way.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Milošević</a> was overthrown in the protests of 5 October 2000 after losing the election to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojislav-kostunica">Vojislav Koštunica</a>, and was later sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>. The EU-brokered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> Agreement of 2002 replaced the federation with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia-and-montenegro">Serbia and Montenegro</a> in February 2003.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia.jpg" alt="Federal Republic of Yugoslavia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. (www.gov.yu) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Serbia_and_Montenegro_(1992%E2%80%932006).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1992 CE – 2003 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Belgrade</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dobrica Ćosić, Slobodan Milošević, Vojislav Koštunica</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Serbian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>FRY, Rump Yugoslavia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1998&amp;focus=federal-republic-of-yugoslavia" aria-label="Federal Republic of Yugoslavia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5168 594 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Federal Republic of Yugoslavia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5488 850L5488 850M5488 850L5488 850"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5488 850h0M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5488 850h0"/><path class="map